 we spoke  to them earlier they they only speak uh  ukrainian but they were saying about how  they've spent the past couple of days  just sifting through  all of this  um and trying to make sense of what  happened there  they're pretty much in shock to be  honest and very concerned about the  the increasing  what they see and perceive as increasing  attacks  the woman that you see there who's been  going through  all the rubble um had some pretty choice  words for the um the russian president  um when when i asked her about what what  she she thought about was happening in  this area and we know that yesterday we  were in a completely different area  where um civilians who were trying to  flee an active combat zone were being  fired on as they were trying to flee so  there is no civilian at the moment in in  ukraine who feels particularly safe at  the moment there are no international  laws it seems which are being observed  and a lot of  anger and hesitation earlier we were  talking to the former ukrainian  president petra polish poroshenko who  was  on the one hand saying how grateful he  was  to britain america a number of other  countries um in the west who have  rallied to ukraine's support but also  demanding so much more  urging so much more action saying that  he he was incredibly bullish about the  prospects of ukraine  actually succeeding in this um david and  goliath battle you know because the  ukrainian military is pretty much  dwarfed by the russian one and uh the  numbers of of russian soldiers but he  feels that they have the courage the  determination the defiance however  he says they need  so much more they need  aircraft they need the fly the skies to  be closed he's demanding he they want  more weapons they want  much more unified response basically  from the international community and he  had some fairly cautionary and chilling  words about how this you know this isn't  just ukraine's fight this is the whole  world's fight he believes it's a r